The files or variables contain information regarding the configu‐
ration of each network interface.  is processed by at system boot
time.   For each interface that is to be configured, there should
be either an variable in or an file (such as the variable or  the
file  for  the  interface).  Only characters allowed in variables
names should be used for uppercase and lowercase letters, digits,
and underscore The variable or file will get  evaluated  only  if
the interface exists on the system.  Multiple lines can be placed
in  a  variable  or file, and will be evaluated sequentially.  In
the case of a variable, semicolons may be used  instead  of  new‐
lines,  as  described  in sequences in files are ignored, so long
logical lines may be made up of several shorter  physical  lines.
Normally,  a  line will be evaluated as command line arguments to
will be prepended on evaluation.  Arguments with  embedded  shell
metacharacters  should  be quoted in style.  If the line is equal
to will be started for the interface.   However,  it  is  instead
recommended that is set to true in and any per interface configu‐
ration  or restriction is done in If the line is equal to a dedi‐
cated process will be started for processing received router  ad‐
vertisements and sending out IPv6 router solicitation messages on
the  interface.   This is useful on networks where default routes
can best be learned from router advertisements.  However, if  has
been  set  to  true  in it is assumed that that process will take
care of sending any necessary router  solicitation  messages  and
processing  received router advertisements on all interfaces, and
therefore no per‐interface process is  started.   If  a  line  is
empty,  or starts with the line will be ignored as comment.  If a
line starts with the rest of line will  get  evaluated  as  shell
script  fragment.  Shell variables declared in are accessible but
may not be modified.  The most useful variable is as it  will  be
